<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A1.F12.sf4.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(d)</span> </span><span id="A1.F12.sf4.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Annealing (<math id="A1.F12.sf4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda=0.9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.9</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda=0.9</annotation></semantics></math>)</span></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A1.F12.7" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 12</span>. </span><span id="A1.F12.8" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">A 2D flow matching toy example with a target distribution shaped as a wide ring. Random seeds conditioned on <math id="A1.F12.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c=3\pi/4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c=3\pi/4</annotation></semantics></math> are shown, along with their trajectories in gray. The dashed region indicates a tolerance band of <math id="A1.F12.m8" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\pm\pi/64" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>±</mo><mi>π</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>64</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pm\pi/64</annotation></semantics></math> around the target condition <math id="A1.F12.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math>, where the manifold density is high. <span id="A1.F12.8.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(a)</span> <math id="A1.F12.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w=1.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1.0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w=1.0</annotation></semantics></math>: Low guidance scale results in weak condition adherence. <span id="A1.F12.8.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(b)</span> <math id="A1.F12.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w=1.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w=1.5</annotation></semantics></math>: Moderate guidance slightly improves alignment, but some samples still deviate from the target region. <span id="A1.F12.8.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(c)</span> <math id="A1.F12.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w=2.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2.0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w=2.0</annotation></semantics></math>: Strong guidance leads to overfitting the condition, pulling samples off the true manifold. <span id="A1.F12.8.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(d)</span> Ours: The trained annealing scheduler achieves accurate condition alignment while preserving sample quality.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="A1.F13" class="ltx_figure">
<p id="A1.F13.3" class="ltx_p">][t] 
<span id="A1.F13.3.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_align_middle">
<span id="A1.F13.3.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<span id="A1.F13.3.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center"><img src="2506.24108v1/diff1.png" id="A1.F13.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:181/162;" width="181" height="162" alt="Refer to caption"></span>
<span id="A1.F13.3.1.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center"><img src="2506.24108v1/diff_24.png" id="A1.F13.g2" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:151/156;" width="151" height="156" alt="Refer to caption"></span>
<span id="A1.F13.3.1.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center"><img src="2506.24108v1/diff_49.png" id="A1.F13.g3" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:151/156;" width="151" height="156" alt="Refer to caption"></span></span>
<span id="A1.F13.3.1.2" class="ltx_tr">
<span id="A1.F13.3.1.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.F13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=1</annotation></semantics></math></span>
<span id="A1.F13.3.1.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.F13.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=0.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=0.5</annotation></semantics></math></span>
<span id="A1.F13.3.1.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.F13.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=0</annotation></semantics></math></span></span>
</span></p>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A1.F13.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 13</span>. </span><math id="A1.F13.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log||\delta_{t}||" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em" stretchy="true">‖</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">δ</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">t</mi></msub><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em" stretchy="true">‖</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log||\delta_{t}||</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A1.F13.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;"> heatmap for <math id="A1.F13.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c=3\pi/4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c=3\pi/4</annotation></semantics></math>.<span id="A1.F13.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> This quantity illustrates how well unconditional and conditional predictions align in magnitude and direction across the latent space for varying timesteps. The region between the black circles marks areas of high sample density, while the blue dashed line represents the desired conditional region. <math id="A1.F13.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=1</annotation></semantics></math>: At large <math id="A1.F13.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>’s, noise dominates the samples, with model predictions tending toward the center of the source distribution, albeit slightly noisy. <math id="A1.F13.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=0.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=0.5</annotation></semantics></math>: As <math id="A1.F13.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> increases, unconditional and conditional predictions align more closely, showing lower values near the desired condition, but even lower values remain farther from the distribution in the direction of <math id="A1.F13.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math>. <math id="A1.F13.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=1</annotation></semantics></math>: By the final timestep, a local minimum is achieved at the target location within the ring.</span></span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS9" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.9. </span>Extension to Flow Matching</h3>

<div id="A1.SS9.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS9.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our scheduler can be naturally extended to flow-based models by leveraging the continuous-time formulation of Flow Matching <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lipman et al., 2022</a>)</cite>. In this setting, we model the trajectory of samples <math id="A1.SS9.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x(t)</annotation></semantics></math> using a learned velocity field <math id="A1.SS9.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{\theta}(x,t,c)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{\theta}(x,t,c)</annotation></semantics></math>, governed by the ordinary differential equation:</p>
<table id="A1.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{dx}{dt}=v_{\theta}(x,t,c)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{dx}{dt}=v_{\theta}(x,t,c),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS9.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A1.SS9.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the conditioning signal. The model is trained to match the true velocity <math id="A1.SS9.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{1}-x_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{1}-x_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> by sampling intermediate points <math id="A1.SS9.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x(t)=x_{0}+t(x_{1}-x_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x(t)=x_{0}+t(x_{1}-x_{0})</annotation></semantics></math> and minimizing a velocity prediction loss, analogous to the diffusion-based <math id="A1.SS9.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>-prediction loss. Specifically, the equivalent of the <math id="A1.SS9.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>-loss becomes:</p>
<table id="A1.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\epsilon}=\left\|v_{\theta}(x(t),t,c)-(x_{1}-x_{0})\right\|^{2}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>ϵ</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\epsilon}=\left\|v_{\theta}(x(t),t,c)-(x_{1}-x_{0})\right\|^{2}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS9.p1.3" class="ltx_p">Furthermore, we define a <math id="A1.SS9.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math>-loss similar to the diffusion model case. After an integration step to a future point <math id="A1.SS9.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x(t+\Delta t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x(t+\Delta t)</annotation></semantics></math>, we compute the discrepancy between the conditional and unconditional velocity predictions:</p>
<table id="A1.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{t+\Delta{t}}=v_{\theta}(x(t+\Delta t),t+\Delta t,c)-v_{\theta}(x(t+\Delta t),t+\Delta t,\emptyset)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{t+\Delta{t}}=v_{\theta}(x(t+\Delta t),t+\Delta t,c)-v_{\theta}(x(t+\Delta t),t+\Delta t,\emptyset),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS9.p1.4" class="ltx_p">and define the loss as:</p>
<table id="A1.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\delta}=\left\|\delta_{t+\Delta{t}}\right\|^{2}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>δ</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\delta}=\left\|\delta_{t+\Delta{t}}\right\|^{2}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS9.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS9.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Similarly to the toy example presented for diffusion models, we train a 2D toy flow matching model that predicts the conditional velocity field <math id="A1.SS9.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{\theta}(x,t,\{c,\varnothing\})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{\theta}(x,t,\{c,\varnothing\})</annotation></semantics></math>, and afterwards train an annealing scheduler using the equivalent velocity matching objectives described above (<math id="A1.SS9.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\delta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>δ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\delta}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.SS9.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\epsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>ϵ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\epsilon}</annotation></semantics></math>). At inference time, as a baseline, we apply guidance by combining the conditional and unconditional velocity predictions using a scaled interpolation (namely the guidance scale <math id="A1.SS9.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math>), and finally present our scheduler guidance. For the target condition <math id="A1.SS9.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c=3\pi/4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c=3\pi/4</annotation></semantics></math>, we visualize the resulting trajectories and sample alignments in Figure <a href="#A1.F12" title="Figure 12 ‣ A.8. Other Solvers &amp; Noise Schedules ‣ Appendix A Supplementary Material ‣ Navigating with Annealing Guidance Scale in Diffusion Space" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a>. The results show that our proposed annealing-based scheduler achieves better condition alignment and sample quality compared to constant guidance scales, confirming the effectiveness of our approach in the flow matching setting as well.
To further analyze the behavior of the model, we visualize the quantity <math id="A1.SS9.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|\delta_{t}\|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\delta_{t}\|</annotation></semantics></math> across different timesteps. As shown in Figure <a href="#A1.F13" title="Figure 13 ‣ A.8. Other Solvers &amp; Noise Schedules ‣ Appendix A Supplementary Material ‣ Navigating with Annealing Guidance Scale in Diffusion Space" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a>, we observe a pattern similar to that in the diffusion model, highlighting the desirability of low <math id="A1.SS9.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|\delta_{t}\|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\delta_{t}\|</annotation></semantics></math> values, which indicate the desirability of better agreement between conditional and unconditional predictions.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS10" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.10. </span>Additional Results</h3>

<div id="A1.SS10.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS10.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We present additional qualitative comparisons to further highlight the differences between methods.
Figures <a href="#A1.F14" title="Figure 14 ‣ A.10. Additional Results ‣ Appendix A Supplementary Material ‣ Navigating with Annealing Guidance Scale in Diffusion Space" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14</span></a> and <a href="#A1.F15" title="Figure 15 ‣ A.10. Additional Results ‣ Appendix A Supplementary Material ‣ Navigating with Annealing Guidance Scale in Diffusion Space" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">15</span></a> show comparisons against CFG, while Figs. <a href="#A1.F16" title="Figure 16 ‣ A.10. Additional Results ‣ Appendix A Supplementary Material ‣ Navigating with Annealing Guidance Scale in Diffusion Space" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">16</span></a> and <a href="#A1.F17" title="Figure 17 ‣ A.10. Additional Results ‣ Appendix A Supplementary Material ‣ Navigating with Annealing Guidance Scale in Diffusion Space" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">17</span></a> present comparisons against CFG++.</p>
</div>
